1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
SpoofLab: Testing Presence Verification Behind Optical Pulse Sensors

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,500
SmartSpectra measures heart rate and breathing accurately from facial video.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
However, a recorded video of a face still contains a genuine optical pulse.

4
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:18,500
Detecting a pulse does not prove a live person is physically present.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,500
SpoofLab is an experimental workbench to test decision rules and trade-offs.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Part 1: Camera Frame Rate & Calibration

7
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,500
Our 6-second camera diagnostic verifies frame rate >= 25 FPS for the SDK.

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Part 2: Live Baseline Capture

9
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:41,500
After confirming consent, SmartSpectra locks onto live optical blood flow.

10
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Pulse rate and breathing update in real time with active sparklines.

11
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,500
The 12-second warm-up is strictly excluded to prevent startup bias.

12
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Part 3: The Replay Dilemma

13
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:00,500
We test 4 inputs: Live, Printed Photo, Screen Replay, and Digital Video.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,500
When replaying a video on a screen, the sensor still detects a pulse.

15
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,500
A naive rule that only checks "pulse detected" passes screen replays.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Part 4: Active Freshness Challenge

17
00:01:15,500 --> 00:01:19,500
To solve this, SpoofLab issues randomized micro-challenges.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The user is prompted to blink an exact number of times during capture.

19
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:28,500
A live user responds in real time; a static replay cannot predict the prompt.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,500
Part 5: Policy Arena — Blind Evaluation

21
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:38,000
The Policy Arena evaluates three frozen rules with zero metadata leakage.

22
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:42,500
The verifier only sees numerical stability and validation metrics.

23
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:47,000
We evaluate the trial blindly, then click "Reveal" to flip the card.

24
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:52,000
Every rule moves the trade-off: stricter gates cut replays but fail live users.

25
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:56,500
Part 6: Trust Boundaries Architecture

26
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,000
We enforce separation: Measurement -> Policy -> Verdict.

27
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,500
A physiological signal is sensor data, not an authentication claim.

28
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Part 7: Evidence Ledger (33 Trials)

29
00:02:10,500 --> 00:02:14,500
All 33 attempts remain in the ledger, including 10 inconclusive trials.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,000
Never silently drop inconclusive trials—retaining them ensures an honest audit.

31
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:24,000
Our matrix uses Wilson confidence intervals across all retained attempts.

32
00:02:24,500 --> 00:02:28,500
Part 8: Model Lab Limits Analysis

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Instead of claiming inflated ML accuracy, we ran an honest limits analysis.

34
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:37,500
Our 50-run shuffled label control matched real fits 8 times.

35
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,500
Leave-one-group-out cross-validation yielded an AUC interval spanning chance.

36
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,500
Summary features alone cannot generalize without active freshness challenges.

37
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Part 9: Privacy & Open Source

38
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,500
SpoofLab is 100% local: zero cloud telemetry and zero video recording.

39
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,500
All local evidence can be purged with a single click in Export.

40
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,500
Open source on GitHub: github.com/Pritam-mb/presage

41
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Break the assumptions, not the sensor.
